{0 Tutorial}

This tutorial builds parsers for small languages in steps, starting from a
single assignment and ending with error recovery and token streams. Every
snippet is complete OCaml and every error message shown is real output. The
snippets are meant to be read in order: later ones reuse earlier definitions.

{1 A first parser}

Everything is in the top level of the {!Parkour} module. A parser is a value of
type ['a Parkour.t]; {!Parkour.parse} runs it on a string and requires it to
consume all of it. On failure you get a list of {!Parkour.Diagnostic.t}, which
are data (position, expected, found, ...) that {!Parkour.Diagnostic.render}
turns into a report against a {!Parkour.Source.t}.

{[
open Parkour

let run show p input =
  match parse p input with
  | Ok v -> print_endline (show v)
  | Error diagnostics ->
      let source = Source.make ~name:"input" input in
      List.iter
        (fun d -> print_string (Diagnostic.render source d))
        diagnostics

let assignment =
  let+ name = take_while1 "name" is_letter
  and+ () = skip_while is_space *> char '=' *> skip_while is_space
  and+ value = integer in
  (name, value)

let show (name, value) = Printf.sprintf "%s = %d" name value
let () = run show assignment "ammo = 42"
let () = run show assignment "ammo = banana"
let () = run show assignment "ammo 42"
]}

{v
ammo = 42
error: expected integer, found "banana"
 --> input:1:8
  |
1 | ammo = banana
  |        ^^^^^^ expected integer
error: expected '=', found "42"
 --> input:1:6
  |
1 | ammo 42
  |      ^^ expected '='
v}

Nothing in the grammar says what to report: each primitive knows what it
expects ({!Parkour.integer} expects [integer], {!Parkour.char} expects the
character), and the failure that got furthest into the input is the one shown.
The name given to {!Parkour.take_while1} is what appears in messages when there
is no such token.

{1 Building blocks}

Parsers are combined with [let+] and [and+] (several parsers in a row, as
above), [*>] and [<*] (sequence, keep the right or left result), and [>>|] or
{!Parkour.map} (transform the result). {!Parkour.many}, {!Parkour.sep_by} and
the other repetitions produce lists, and {!Parkour.between} wraps a parser in
delimiters:

{[
let list_of_ints =
  between (char '[') (char ']') (sep_by ~sep:(char ',') integer)

let show_list l = String.concat ";" (List.map string_of_int l)
let () = run show_list list_of_ints "[1,2,3]"
let () = run show_list list_of_ints "[1,2,3"
]}

{v
1;2;3
error: expected ',' or ']', found end of input
 --> input:1:7
  |
1 | [1,2,3
  | - opened here
  |       ^ expected ',' or ']'
v}

The ["opened here"] label is added by {!Parkour.between} when the closing
delimiter is missing, so an unclosed bracket points back to where it began.
The error also shows that failures at the same position are merged: after an
element, either [,] or [\]] could continue.

{1 Whitespace and tokens}

Spelling out whitespace by hand does not scale. {!Parkour.Lexeme.make} builds
the parsers for a language's lexical conventions: comments, identifiers and
reserved words. Each of its parsers skips the whitespace and comments that
follow the token, so a grammar written with them never mentions whitespace,
except to skip the leading space once:

{[
module L = (val Lexeme.make ~line_comments:[ "#" ] ())

let assignment =
  let+ name = L.identifier
  and+ () = L.punct "="
  and+ value = L.lexeme integer
  and+ () = L.punct ";" in
  (name, value)

let program = L.space *> many assignment

let show_program l =
  String.concat ", " (List.map (fun (n, v) -> Printf.sprintf "%s=%d" n v) l)

let () = run show_program program "ammo = 42; # rounds\n  health = 100;\n"
let () = run show_program program "ammo = 42;\nhealth = ;\n"
let () = run show_program program "ammo = 42;\nhealth = 100\n"
]}

{v
ammo=42, health=100
error: expected integer, found ';'
 --> input:2:10
  |
2 | health = ;
  |          ^ expected integer
error: expected ';', found end of input
 --> input:3:1
  |
3 |
  | ^ expected ';'
v}

[L.symbol s] matches [s] and returns it; [L.punct s] is the same but returns
[()], for punctuation whose text is not needed (hence [and+ () = ...] above).
[L.keyword s] matches a whole word (so [keyword "if"] rejects [iffy]);
[L.identifier] reads a name that is not in the [~reserved] list; [L.lexeme p] is
any parser [p] followed by whitespace. {!Parkour.hidden} keeps a parser out of
the [expected ...] lists, and is what makes the whitespace skipping invisible in
messages.

{1 Choice and commitment}

[p <|> q] (or {!Parkour.choice}) is ordered like a PEG: [p] runs first, and if
it fails, [q] runs from the same position, however much input [p] consumed. The
first alternative that succeeds wins, and a later failure never goes back to try
the other alternative differently.

Backtracking freely has a cost, and it is that a mistake in one alternative can
be explained away by another. In this language [let] is not reserved, so
[let;] is a perfectly good call of a function named [let] (the module [L] is
the one defined above):

{[
let semi = L.punct ";"

let declaration =
  let+ _ = L.keyword "let"
  and+ name = L.identifier
  and+ () = L.punct "="
  and+ n = L.lexeme integer
  and+ () = semi in
  Printf.sprintf "declare %s = %d" name n

let call =
  let+ name = L.identifier and+ () = semi in
  "call " ^ name

let statement = declaration <|> call
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let 5;"
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let;"
]}

{v
error: expected identifier or ';', found '5'
 --> input:1:5
  |
1 | let 5;
  |     ^ expected identifier or ';'
call let
v}

Neither answer is what the author meant. Once the parser has seen the keyword
[let], it knows which construct is intended; {!Parkour.cut} commits to it. After
a cut, the enclosing alternatives (and repetitions and options) can no longer
backtrack past it, and a failure is reported as is:

{[
let declaration =
  let+ _ = L.keyword "let" <* cut
  and+ name = L.identifier
  and+ () = L.punct "="
  and+ n = L.lexeme integer
  and+ () = semi in
  Printf.sprintf "declare %s = %d" name n

let statement = declaration <|> call
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let 5;"
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let;"
]}

{v
error: expected identifier, found '5'
 --> input:1:5
  |
1 | let 5;
  |     ^ expected identifier
error: expected identifier, found ';'
 --> input:1:4
  |
1 | let;
  |    ^ expected identifier
v}

A good rule: put a [cut] right after the token that identifies a construct
(a keyword, an opening bracket, the [=] of an assignment).

{!Parkour.attempt} is the opposite: commitments made inside it do not escape,
so enclosing alternatives may backtrack over it again. Use it sparingly, when a
construct really has a committing prefix that is also valid on its own:

{[
let statement = attempt declaration <|> call
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let;"
]}

{v
call let
v}

Two combinators change how failures are described rather than how parsing goes.
{!Parkour.label} replaces the expectations of a parser that fails without
consuming input, and {!Parkour.context} records which construct was being
parsed when something failed after its first token:

{[
let statement = declaration <|> call
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "5;"

let statement = label "statement" (declaration <|> call)
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "5;"

let statement = context "declaration" declaration <|> call
let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> statement) "let x 5;"
]}

{v
error: expected identifier or "let", found '5'
 --> input:1:1
  |
1 | 5;
  | ^ expected identifier or "let"
error: expected statement, found '5'
 --> input:1:1
  |
1 | 5;
  | ^ expected statement
error: expected '=', found '5'
 --> input:1:7
  |
1 | let x 5;
  |       ^ expected '='
  = while parsing declaration, from 1:1
v}

{1 Recursion}

A recursive grammar needs a way to refer to itself before it is defined.
{!Parkour.fix} does it for one parser; for several that refer to each other use
[let rec ... = lazy ...] and {!Parkour.force}:

{[
let rec value =
  lazy
    (choice ~label:"value"
       [ (L.lexeme natural >>| string_of_int); force list ])

and list =
  lazy
    (between (L.symbol "[") (L.symbol "]") (sep_by ~sep:(L.symbol ",") (force value))
     >>| fun items -> "[" ^ String.concat " " items ^ "]")

let () = run Fun.id (force value) "[1, [2, 3], []]"
]}

{v
[1 [2 3] []]
v}

Repetitions are loops, so long inputs do not use the stack. Recursion does, once
per level of nesting, so Parkour counts the entries into {!Parkour.fix},
{!Parkour.delay} and {!Parkour.force}: past [max_depth] (default
{!Parkour.default_max_depth}) the parse stops with a [nesting too deep] error
instead of a stack overflow. Hostile input such as ten thousand opening
brackets is therefore an ordinary error. The limit is an optional argument of
{!Parkour.parse}.

{1 Operators}

{!Parkour.expression} builds an expression parser from a table of operators,
listed from the tightest binding level to the loosest. Combined with
{!Parkour.fix}, {!Parkour.between} and {!Parkour.sep_by}, a small expression
language with function calls takes a page:

{[
type expr =
  | Num of int
  | Var of string
  | Call of string * expr list
  | Neg of expr
  | Bin of string * expr * expr

let rec show_expr = function
  | Num n -> string_of_int n
  | Var v -> v
  | Call (f, args) -> f ^ "(" ^ String.concat ", " (List.map show_expr args) ^ ")"
  | Neg e -> "-" ^ show_expr e
  | Bin (op, a, b) -> "(" ^ show_expr a ^ " " ^ op ^ " " ^ show_expr b ^ ")"

let operator op f = hidden (L.symbol op) <* cut >>| fun _ -> f
let binary op = operator op (fun a b -> Bin (op, a, b))

let expr =
  fix (fun expr ->
      let args =
        between (L.symbol "(") (L.symbol ")") (sep_by ~sep:(L.symbol ",") expr)
      in
      let atom =
        choice ~label:"expression"
          [
            (let+ n = L.lexeme natural in
             Num n);
            (let+ name = L.identifier and+ args = optional args in
             match args with None -> Var name | Some args -> Call (name, args));
            between (L.symbol "(") (L.symbol ")") expr;
          ]
      in
      expression atom
        [
          [ Prefix (operator "-" (fun e -> Neg e)) ];
          [ Infix_left (binary "*"); Infix_left (binary "/") ];
          [ Infix_left (binary "+"); Infix_left (binary "-") ];
        ])

let full = L.space *> expr
let () = run show_expr full "1 + 2 * x"
let () = run show_expr full "-a * max(1, b + 2) - 3"
let () = run show_expr full "(1 + 2"
let () = run show_expr full "max(1, 2"
let () = run show_expr full "1 + * 2"
]}

{v
(1 + (2 * x))
((-a * max(1, (b + 2))) - 3)
error: expected ')', found end of input
 --> input:1:7
  |
1 | (1 + 2
  | - opened here
  |       ^ expected ')'
error: expected ')' or ',', found end of input
 --> input:1:9
  |
1 | max(1, 2
  |    - opened here
  |         ^ expected ')' or ','
error: expected expression, found '*'
 --> input:1:5
  |
1 | 1 + * 2
  |     ^ expected expression
v}

Two details make these messages good. The operators are {!Parkour.hidden}:
after an operand the error should say what is missing (the [)]) rather than list
every operator that could have continued the expression. And each operator has a
[cut] after its token, so that in [1 + * 2] the error is the missing operand,
not a [*] that was found where a [+] could have ended the expression. The
{{!Parkour.operator}operator} type also has [Postfix], [Infix_right] and
[Infix_none] (non-associative: [a < b < c] is rejected).

{1 Custom errors}

Not every mistake is a syntax error. {!Parkour.validate} checks the result of a
parser, and the failure underlines everything the parser consumed:

{[
let port =
  validate
    (fun n -> if n < 1 || n > 65535 then Error "port out of range" else Ok n)
    (L.lexeme natural)

let setting =
  let+ key = L.identifier and+ () = L.punct "=" <* cut and+ p = port in
  (key, p)

let show_setting (k, p) = Printf.sprintf "%s = %d" k p
let () = run show_setting setting "port = 80"
let () = run show_setting setting "port = 70000"
]}

{v
port = 80
error: port out of range
 --> input:1:8
  |
1 | port = 70000
  |        ^^^^^
v}

For anything else there is {!Parkour.fail}, with [~span] to underline something
other than the current position, [~labels] for secondary spans, and [~notes] for
hints. {!Parkour.spanned} supplies the spans:

{[
let name =
  let* word, span = spanned L.identifier in
  if List.mem word [ "if"; "let" ] then
    fail ~span
      ~notes:[ "reserved words cannot be used as names" ]
      (Printf.sprintf "'%s' is a reserved word" word)
  else return word

let () = run Fun.id (L.space *> name) "let"

let pair =
  let* a, first = spanned L.identifier in
  let* b, second = spanned L.identifier in
  if a = b then
    fail ~span:second ~labels:[ (first, "first defined here") ] "duplicate name"
  else return (a ^ b)

let () = run Fun.id pair "x x"
]}

{v
error: 'let' is a reserved word
 --> input:1:1
  |
1 | let
  | ^^^
  = note: reserved words cannot be used as names
error: duplicate name
 --> input:1:3
  |
1 | x x
  | - first defined here
  |   ^
v}

A custom message takes precedence over plain expectations at the same position,
and [fail] does not commit.

{1 Error recovery}

By default parsing stops at the first error. Recovery is opt-in, for tools that
want to report every problem in one run, and uses these pieces:

- {!Parkour.recover} [~sync ~fallback p]: if [p] fails (by default only after a
  [cut]), the error is recorded, the [sync] parser skips ahead to a safe place,
  and [fallback] provides a placeholder value;
- {!Parkour.skip_past} and {!Parkour.skip_to}: the usual [sync] parsers, which skip
  to a terminator, optionally tracking nested delimiters with
  [~nested:(open_, close)];
- {!Parkour.or_insert}: for a missing terminator, records the error and carries
  on as if the token were there, without skipping anything;
- {!Parkour.parse_partial}: returns the value, if any, together with all the
  diagnostics recorded. ({!Parkour.parse} returns [Error] whenever an error was
  recorded.)

A file of [key = value;] settings where every broken setting is reported and
skipped:

{[
type value = Int of int | Str of string

let value =
  choice ~label:"value"
    [
      (let+ n = L.lexeme integer in
       Int n);
      (let+ s = L.lexeme string_literal in
       Str s);
    ]

let setting =
  let+ key = L.identifier
  and+ () = L.punct "=" <* cut
  and+ v = value
  and+ () = or_insert () (L.punct ";") in
  (key, v)

let sync = skip_past ~nested:(char '{', char '}') (char ';') *> L.space

let item =
  recover ~catch:`Any ~sync ~fallback:(fun _ -> None) (setting >>| Option.some)

let file = L.space *> many item <* eof

let show_value = function
  | Int n -> string_of_int n
  | Str s -> Printf.sprintf "%S" s

let input =
  {|name = "demo";
port = ;
retries = 3
debug = 1;
= 5;
|}

let () =
  let source = Source.make ~name:"demo.conf" input in
  let result, diagnostics = parse_partial file input in
  List.iter (fun d -> print_string (Diagnostic.render source d)) diagnostics;
  List.iter
    (function
      | Some (k, v) -> Printf.printf "%s = %s\n" k (show_value v)
      | None -> print_endline "(skipped)")
    (Option.value result ~default:[])
]}

{v
error: expected value, found ';'
 --> demo.conf:2:8
  |
2 | port = ;
  |        ^ expected value
error: expected ';', found "debug"
 --> demo.conf:3:12
  |
3 | retries = 3
  |            ^ expected ';'
4 | debug = 1;
  | -----
error: expected identifier, found '='
 --> demo.conf:5:1
  |
5 | = 5;
  | ^ expected identifier
name = "demo"
(skipped)
retries = 3
debug = 1
(skipped)
v}

Note how the missing [;] is reported after [3], where it belongs, and
[retries = 3] is still read: [or_insert] does not discard anything. [recover]
has [~catch:`Any] here since no alternative should run for a broken setting;
without it only failures after a [cut] are recovered. The number of recorded
errors is capped by [~max_errors] (default 100), and parsing carries on to the
end past the cap.

{!Parkour.emit} records a diagnostic of your own and carries on. A warning does
not make {!Parkour.parse} fail:

{[
let setting_with_warning =
  let* key, span = spanned L.identifier in
  let* () =
    if key <> String.lowercase_ascii key then
      emit (Diagnostic.make ~severity:`Warning ~span "keys should be lowercase")
    else return ()
  in
  let+ () = L.punct "=" and+ v = value in
  (key, v)

let () =
  let input = "Port = 80" in
  let source = Source.make ~name:"demo.conf" input in
  match parse_partial setting_with_warning input with
  | Some (k, v), diagnostics ->
      List.iter (fun d -> print_string (Diagnostic.render source d)) diagnostics;
      Printf.printf "%s = %s\n" k (show_value v)
  | None, _ -> ()
]}

{v
warning: keys should be lowercase
 --> demo.conf:1:1
  |
1 | Port = 80
  | ^^^^
Port = 80
v}

The documentation of {!Parkour.recover} has more guidance: put it around items
that start with a [cut], use one at each nesting level of a block, and prefer
[or_insert] for terminators. The [examples/] directory has two larger
recovering parsers ([config.ml], [mini.ml]).

{1 Token input}

Large grammars are often easier in two steps: a lexer turns the text into
tokens, then the grammar works on the tokens and never sees whitespace or
comments. {!Parkour.tokenize} lexes with ordinary parsers (the [~skip] parser
runs between tokens and is where whitespace goes), and {!Parkour.Tokens} gives
the combinators over an array of tokens. Diagnostics still point into the
original text, since every token carries its span:

{[
type tok = Num of int | Name of string | Sym of string

module Tok = struct
  type t = tok

  let equal = ( = )

  let show = function
    | Num n -> string_of_int n
    | Name s | Sym s -> s
end

module T = Parkour.Tokens (Tok)

let lexer =
  let token =
    choice ~label:"token"
      [
        (let+ n = natural in
         Num n);
        (let+ s = take_while1 "name" is_letter in
         Name s);
        (let+ c = one_of "=+;" in
         Sym (String.make 1 c));
      ]
  in
  tokenize ~skip:(skip_while is_space) token

let statement =
  let open T in
  let name = expect "name" (function Name s -> Some s | _ -> None) in
  let num = expect "number" (function Num n -> Some n | _ -> None) in
  let+ x = name
  and+ _ = token (Sym "=")
  and+ n = chainl1 num (token (Sym "+") *> return ( + ))
  and+ _ = token (Sym ";") in
  (x, n)

let run_tokens text =
  let source = Source.make ~name:"input" text in
  let report ds =
    List.iter (fun d -> print_string (Diagnostic.render source d)) ds
  in
  match lexer text with
  | Error ds -> report ds
  | Ok tokens -> (
      match T.parse statement (T.input ~source:text tokens) with
      | Ok (x, n) -> Printf.printf "%s = %d\n" x n
      | Error ds -> report ds)

let () = run_tokens "total = 1 + 2  +  39;"
let () = run_tokens "total = 1 + ;"
let () = run_tokens "total = 1 ? 2;"
]}

{v
total = 42
error: expected number, found ';'
 --> input:1:13
  |
1 | total = 1 + ;
  |             ^ expected number
error: expected token or end of input, found '?'
 --> input:1:11
  |
1 | total = 1 ? 2;
  |           ^ expected token or end of input
v}

[examples/mini.ml] and [examples/mini_tokens.ml] parse the same small language
both ways and print the same errors.

{1 Performance notes}

The defaults are fast enough for configuration files and small languages. A few
habits keep larger grammars quick:

- Prefer {!Parkour.dispatch} over {!Parkour.choice} when many alternatives start
  with different tokens. [dispatch] looks at the next token and runs one parser
  directly, instead of trying each alternative in turn. When the alternatives'
  first tokens are disjoint it gives the same results and diagnostics as the
  [choice] with the same label.
- Skip whitespace after tokens with {!Parkour.Lexeme} (or a {!Parkour.hidden}
  parser), not before each of them, so it is skipped exactly once.
- Avoid wide {!Parkour.attempt}s: when what it wraps fails late, the next
  alternative parses the same input again. Commit with {!Parkour.cut} and keep
  [attempt] to the few tokens that decide.
- Use {!Parkour.fold_many} or {!Parkour.skip_many} when the list of results is
  not needed.

{[
type json = Str of string | Int of int | List of json list | Bool of bool

let json =
  fix (fun json ->
      let list =
        L.symbol "[" *> cut *> sep_by ~sep:(L.symbol ",") json
        <* L.symbol "]"
        >>| fun l -> List l
      in
      dispatch ~label:"value" (function
        | '"' -> Some (L.lexeme string_literal >>| fun s -> Str s)
        | '[' -> Some list
        | 't' -> Some (L.keyword "true" >>| fun _ -> Bool true)
        | '-' | '0' .. '9' -> Some (L.lexeme integer >>| fun n -> Int n)
        | _ -> None))

let rec show_json = function
  | Str s -> Printf.sprintf "%S" s
  | Int n -> string_of_int n
  | Bool b -> string_of_bool b
  | List l -> "[" ^ String.concat ", " (List.map show_json l) ^ "]"

let () = run show_json (L.space *> json) {|[1, "two", [true]]|}
let () = run show_json (L.space *> json) {|[1, ?]|}
]}

{v
[1, "two", [true]]
error: expected value, found '?'
 --> input:1:5
  |
1 | [1, ?]
  |     ^ expected value
v}

{1 Where next}

The {!Parkour} module documents every combinator and states the semantics of
commitment, error merging and recovery precisely. The [examples/] directory has
complete programs with galleries of broken inputs: [json.ml], [csv.ml],
[values.ml], [calc.ml], [config.ml] and the two [mini] variants.
